<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sextus Pompey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 67–35 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Sextus Pompeius Magnus Pius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sextus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> was a Roman general, the younger son of Pompey the Great. After the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> he built a naval power based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, sheltering opponents of the triumvirs and cutting off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s grain supply. Octavian's admiral Agrippa defeated him at Naulochus in 36 BCE, and he was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> in 35 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sextus-pompey.jpg" alt="Sextus Pompey" width="320" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dimka Totskiy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aureus_Sextus_Pompeius_42BC_Goldberg_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sextus Pompeius Magnus Pius was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> by his third wife, Mucia Tertia, with an elder brother, Gnaeus Pompeius, and a sister, Pompeia. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> crossed the Rubicon in 49 BCE, Gnaeus followed their father to the East while Sextus stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the care of his stepmother, Cornelia Metella. After the defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pharsalus">Battle of Pharsalus</a> in 48 BCE the two met Pompey on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and fled with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where Sextus watched his father killed by treachery on 29 September of that year.</p><p>He then joined the resistance to Caesar in the African provinces alongside Metellus Scipio, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-younger">Cato the Younger</a> and his brother. Caesar defeated the Pompeius brothers at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-munda">Battle of Munda</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> in 45 BCE. Gnaeus died soon afterwards in a last stand at Lauro; Sextus escaped and raised another army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Caesar's death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> moved that Sextus be recalled, paid 50 million Attic drachmas for his father's confiscated property and given command of the whole Roman navy. He accepted, but went no further than Massilia before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> began its proscriptions, many of the proscribed fled to him, and after the battles of Philippi in 42 BCE survivors of the party of Brutus and Cassius did the same. With the whole island as his base he built an army and a strong navy.</p><p>In 40 BCE his admiral, the freedman Menas, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> from Octavian's governor Marcus Lurius. By the Pact of Misenum in 39 BCE the triumvirs recognised his control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and Sardinia in exchange for keeping the grain supply to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> open and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> in check. The peace was short. Each side accused the other of breaking it, and Menas betrayed Sardinia to Octavian. Sextus defeated Octavian at sea off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> in 37 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa</a> spent a winter building and training a fleet near Lake Avernus. He fought Sextus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mylae">Mylae</a> in August 36 BCE, Marcus Aemilius Lepidus and Titus Statilius Taurus invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and after Naulochus Sextus fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. Captured in 35 BCE, he was put to death without trial at Miletus by Marcus Titius, whom he had once spared, either on Titius's own initiative or possibly on the orders of Antony or Plancus. Plutarch gives him a minor role in the fall of the Republic; Appian treats him as a central figure who might even have emerged as the final victor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sextus Pompey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Off Naulochus in Sicily, Octavian's admiral Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa defeated the fleet of <b>Sextus Pompey</b>, son of Pompey the Great and last leader of organised opposition to the Second Triumvirate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-3">3 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Octavian later made it a colony after it sided with <b>Sextus Pompeius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tauromenium">Tauromenium</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Miletus, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-47" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sextus Pompey"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-47">What was here in 47 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
